As if there's something wrong with wanting to know these things, as an
avid player of the game waiting for bugfixes and improvements.  But
no; it is far from "only" me, as you well know.

My observation about the quality and quantity of programming work done
across the internet is not limited to for-profit projects.  I'm
speaking about the vast amount of volunteer work done on places like,
again, sourceforge.


>Personally, I am very content with the way a new version just appears
>out of thin air now and then, and I have always been pleasantly
>surprised at the new features.


Then I would say you're a very atypical software user and consumer.


>Actually, the multiplicity of new projects *does* make the vanilla game
>better. Most of the important changes in the current game were first
>developed in one of the new projects or posted on this newsgroup. The
>new magic system, for example, wasn't developed by the Dev Team, but by
>someone else, and integrated in the main game, with some changes for
>balance.


So then "the devteam" is doing even less work than previously
supposed, if most of the important game changes have recently been
created by people not on "the devteam".  Imagine the productivity gain
if this broad net already being cast for programming collaboration
were brought out into the open, organized and exposed to all of the
patch and add-on writers out there in a central place.  It would
provide a framework for forward progress that all could see, and again
limit the amount of duplication of effort from patch-writers toiling
in privacy here and there across the internet, often with incompatible
results.

I am not asking any individual developer to put more hours of their
day into nethack than they already are; I'm not asking for "the
devteam" to work more.  I'm asking for the veil of secrecy to be
lifted and thereby make all the work of all the source divers more
organized and useful.  This is an extremely reasonable idea, and one
that is followed in well-run development projects!

In a typical company setting, does development typically follow the
"devteam" method?  Employees A, B, and C are the company "devteam",
and employees D, E, F, G, H, I, J, K, and L work on updates and
snippets of code here and there, doing whatever takes their fancy,
then submitting the work to employees A, B, and C, who then accept and
reject the work of D, E, F, G, H, I, J, K, and L according to rules
and decisions that A, B, and C keep to themselves?  If not, why
doesn't development typically work that way?


>The original author of the Wizard Patch was meanwhile incorporated in
>the Dev Team, if I am not mistaken. By sometimes incorporating new
>members, the Dev Team ensures that they don't get too easy-going. It
>keeps them sharp, I guess.


This seems to happen once in a blue moon.  I believe that a more
transparent collaboration process with some kind of publicized goal,
some kind of release schedule, would keep them much sharper, and
benefit all of us users more.

